---
/**
 * The recommended install, step by step: deploy/install.sh, the installer's options
 * (deploy/installer/lib/args.sh), its checks (preflight.sh) and the bemmoly command
 * (deploy/cli/bemmoly). Tech design §18 is the design behind it.
 */
import CodeBlock from '../../components/site/CodeBlock.astro';
import Section from '../../components/site/Section.astro';
import Transcript from '../../components/site/Transcript.astro';
import Related from '../../components/topics/Related.astro';
import StatusNote from '../../components/topics/StatusNote.astro';
import { RELEASE } from '../../data/landing.ts';
import { pageAt } from '../../data/pages.ts';
import { INSTALL_BLOCK } from '../../data/self-hosting.ts';
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import { INSTALL_HOST, RELEASES_URL } from '../../lib/links.ts';

const OPTIONS = [
  ['--domain NAME', 'The hostname people will use. Asked for when you leave it out.'],
  [
    '--version X.Y.Z',
    'The version to install, or latest or beta. The newest in the channel by default.',
  ],
  ['--channel stable|beta', 'The update channel. Stable by default.'],
  ['--modules a,b', 'Pin the enabled modules. By default an admin chooses them in Settings.'],
  ['--no-proxy', 'Leave out Caddy: you terminate HTTPS, and the app listens on port 8080.'],
  ['--no-in-app-updates', 'Leave out the updater container; update with sudo bemmoly upgrade.'],
  ['--database-url URL', 'Use an existing Postgres 18 instead of the bundled one.'],
  [
    '--image-archive FILE',
    'Load the images from a release bundle instead of pulling them (offline).',
  ],
  ['--dir PATH', 'The install folder. /var/bemmoly by default.'],
  ['--yes', 'Do not ask anything; fail when something is missing.'],
] as const;

const COMMANDS = [
  ['status', 'Containers, version, database size, queue, last backup, certificate.'],
  ['doctor', 'Re-runs the installer’s checks and the app’s health checks, with fixes.'],
  ['logs [service] [-f]', 'Recent logs of bemmoly, db, proxy or updater.'],
  ['backup / backups', 'Take a backup now; list every backup with version, size and verification.'],
  [
    'verify <backup> [--drill]',
    'Check a backup’s checksums, or restore it into a temporary database.',
  ],
  [
    'restore <backup|file|s3-url>',
    'Restore a backup; --mount attaches one read-only beside the live data.',
  ],
  [
    'upgrade [version]',
    'Backup, pull, verify, swap, health check; rolls back on its own if the check fails.',
  ],
  ['rollback', 'Go back to the previous version, saying first exactly what it keeps.'],
  ['config get | set KEY', 'Read or change a setting in .env and restart what uses it.'],
  ['update-channel [stable|beta]', 'Show or change the update channel.'],
] as const;
---

<Page
  page={pageAt('/docs/install')}
  heading="Install Bemmoly on a Linux server"
  lede="One command on a fresh Linux VM installs Docker, Postgres 18, automatic HTTPS, nightly backups and the in-app updater, then prints the address of your workspace."
>
  <StatusNote>
    The installer pulls the newest stable release, {RELEASE.version} today: the foundation (setup,
    people and roles, email, backups and updates), the Work module, with issues, boards, the backlog
    and sprints, and the Docs module, with spaces, a live editor, comments and history.
  </StatusNote>

  <Section title="1. Get a server" id="requirements">
    <ul>
      <li>
        A VM with <strong>2 vCPU and 4 GB</strong> of memory, enough for about 200 people. 2 GB is
        the minimum; <a href="/self-hosting#sizing">sizing</a> covers larger teams.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ubuntu 22.04 or later.</strong> Debian 12, Fedora 40 and Amazon Linux 2023 or later
        are supported by the installer too, but not yet tested end to end as Ubuntu is.
      </li>
      <li>Root or sudo, 10 GB of free disk, ports 80 and 443 free, and outbound DNS and HTTPS.</li>
      <li>
        A domain name with a DNS record pointing at the VM, such as
        <code>work.example.com</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The installer checks each of these itself and stops with the fix for anything missing. If the
      name does not point at the machine yet, it starts with a self-signed certificate, and a real
      one follows by itself once DNS does.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="2. Run the installer" id="run">
    <CodeBlock code={INSTALL_BLOCK} label="On the VM, as root or with sudo" copy />
    <p>
      The script at <code>{INSTALL_HOST}</code> downloads the installer of the matching release from{' '}
      <a href={RELEASES_URL}>GitHub</a>, checks its SHA-256 checksum and runs it with your options.
      To read it before running it, download it first:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      code={`$ curl -fsSL https://${INSTALL_HOST} -o install.sh\n$ less install.sh\n$ sudo sh install.sh --domain work.example.com`}
      copy
    />
    <p>
      Options go after <code>sh -s --</code> when piping, or straight after the script name:
    </p>
    <div tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto rounded-card border border-line">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-14">
        <thead class="bg-side text-12 font-semibold tracking-caps text-tx-3 uppercase dark:bg-card">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-3.5 py-2.5 font-semibold">Option</th>
            <th class="px-3.5 py-2.5 font-semibold">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {OPTIONS.map(([option, body]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-line-2 align-top">
              <td class="px-3.5 py-2.5 whitespace-nowrap">
                <code>{option}</code>
              </td>
              <td class="px-3.5 py-2.5">{body}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="3. Watch what it does" id="what-it-does">
    <Transcript />
    <ul>
      <li>Checks the machine: the operating system, memory, disk, ports and DNS.</li>
      <li>Installs Docker Engine and the Compose plugin if they are missing, or uses yours.</li>
      <li>
        Writes <code>/var/bemmoly</code>: the Compose file, the Caddyfile, a <code>.env</code> with
        freshly generated secrets, and the <code>data</code> and <code>backups</code> folders.
      </li>
      <li>
        Starts the stack. The app waits for Postgres, applies its schema changes under a lock and
        starts; Caddy requests the certificate.
      </li>
      <li>
        Schedules the nightly backup and installs the <code>bemmoly</code> command, then prints the
        address.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section title="4. Open the address and finish setup" id="first-login">
    <p>
      The first visit runs the setup wizard: the server’s health checks, then the first admin
      account, then invitations, what AI may do once it arrives, and the workspace’s look.
      Everything can be changed later in Settings.{' '}
      <a href="/self-hosting#after-install">After install</a> shows the steps.
    </p>
    <p>
      Modules start switched off. To track issues or write pages, turn on Work or Docs in Settings ›
      Modules and choose who can use it: nobody yet, everyone, or specific teams.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep a copy of <code>/var/bemmoly/.env</code> somewhere safe. It holds the key that decrypts
      stored credentials and the backup passphrase, and backups do not contain it.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="5. Look after it with one command" id="after">
    <p>
      The installer puts <code>bemmoly</code> in <code>/usr/local/bin</code>. It asks for sudo
      itself;
      <code>bemmoly help</code> lists everything.
    </p>
    <div tabindex="0" class="overflow-x-auto rounded-card border border-line">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-14">
        <thead class="bg-side text-12 font-semibold tracking-caps text-tx-3 uppercase dark:bg-card">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-3.5 py-2.5 font-semibold">bemmoly …</th>
            <th class="px-3.5 py-2.5 font-semibold">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {COMMANDS.map(([command, body]) => (
            <tr class="border-t border-line-2 align-top">
              <td class="px-3.5 py-2.5 whitespace-nowrap">
                <code>{command}</code>
              </td>
              <td class="px-3.5 py-2.5">{body}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Updates can also run from the app: Settings › Updates shows the release notes and updates with
      one click, taking a backup first. Checking for new releases is off until an admin turns it on
      there.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Other ways to run it" id="other-ways">
    <p>
      Already run Docker Compose? Follow the <a href="/docs/compose">Docker Compose guide</a> with
      the same files the installer writes. An existing Postgres, Kubernetes and servers without
      internet access are covered on <a href="/self-hosting#other-paths">self-hosting</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Related current="/docs/install" />
</Page>
